    story:
      'The story of Kedarnath Jyotirlinga is intertwined with the epic tale of the Mahabharata. After the devastating Kurukshetra war, the Pandavas were overcome with guilt for having killed their own kinsmen, teachers, and grandfathers. They desperately sought absolution from Lord Shiva, who, as the destroyer and transformer, had the power to cleanse them of their terrible sin.\n\nPandavas first came to Varanasi (Kashi) to seek Shiva, but Shiva did not wish to meet them, as he considered their sin of gotra-hatyya (killing of kinsmen) to be very grave. He transformed himself into a bull and hid among the cows and bulls grazing in the Garhwal Himalayan region, in the area now known as Kedarnath. The Pandavas, guided by sages, followed Shiva to this region. As they searched through the herds, Bhima (the strongest Pandava) recognized the unusual bull as Shiva in disguise.\n\nBhima tried to grab the bull, but the bull began to sink into the earth. Bhima held on firmly and managed to grasp its hump as it disappeared into the ground. Shiva, pleased with their perseverance and repentance, appeared before the Pandavas. He agreed to give them darshan and absolve their sins. The hump of the bull that remained above the ground became the Shivalinga worshipped at Kedarnath. The other parts of the bull\'s body manifested at four other locations: the arms at Tungnath, the face at Rudranath, the belly at Madhyamaheshwar, and the locks of hair at Kalpeshwar. These five temples together form the Panch Kedar circuit, all situated in the Garhwal Himalayas.\n\nAdi Shankaracharya, the great 8th-century philosopher and reformer, visited Kedarnath and renovated the ancient temple. It is said that Shankaracharya, after completing his mission of reviving Hinduism across India, returned to Kedarnath in his final days and attained mahasamadhi (conscious departure from the body) here. His samadhi stands behind the main Kedarnath temple to this day. The temple was subsequently maintained and patronized by the kings of the Garhwal region, and today the Uttarakhand Char Dham Devasthanam Management Board oversees its administration.',
    significance:
      'Kedarnath is not only one of the 12 Jyotirlingas but also one of the Char Dhams of Uttarakhand (Chota Char Dham) and part of the Panch Kedar. It is the abode of Sadashiva – the eternal, timeless form of Shiva. A pilgrimage to Kedarnath is believed to grant liberation (moksha) directly. The extreme conditions of the journey – the high altitude, cold temperatures, and arduous trek – are themselves considered a purification process.',
