    story:
      'The story of Bhimashankar Jyotirlinga centers on a mighty demon named Bhima, who was the son of the demon king Kumbhakarna and a woman named Karkati. Kumbhakarna, the brother of Ravana, was slain by Lord Rama in the battle of Lanka. Bhima was born after his father\'s death and grew up without knowing who his father was. His mother raised him in the forest.\n\nWhen Bhima grew up and learned that his father Kumbhakarna had been killed by Rama, and by extension by the Devas (as Rama was an avatar of Vishnu), he was consumed with rage and a desire for revenge against all the gods. He performed intense penance to propitiate Brahma, who granted him tremendous power and near-invincibility. Armed with this boon, Bhima began terrorizing the three worlds, conquering the kingdoms of gods, sages, and humans alike.\n\nBhima captured the Kamrupeshwar, a king who was a devout worshipper of Shiva, and imprisoned him. Despite being imprisoned, Kamrupeshwar continued his worship of Shiva with a clay Shivalinga. When Bhima discovered this, he flew into a rage and attempted to destroy the Shivalinga with his sword. At that moment, Lord Shiva emerged from the Shivalinga in a terrifying form and engaged Bhima in battle. The battle was intense, but Shiva ultimately destroyed Bhima with the heat of his third eye.\n\nAfter destroying the demon, the gods and sages requested Lord Shiva to remain at this spot permanently as a Jyotirlinga to protect the world. Shiva agreed and manifested as Bhimashankar – the Lord who destroyed Bhima. It is said that the sweat from Shiva\'s body during the battle flowed down and formed the sacred Bhima River, which eventually merges with the Krishna River. This river is said to have the power to wash away sins of pilgrims who bathe in it near Bhimashankar.',
