    history:
      'Kashi (Varanasi) is considered the oldest continuously inhabited city in the world and the sacred capital of Shiva. The Kashi Vishwanath temple has been described in numerous ancient scriptures including the Skanda Purana. The original ancient temple was destroyed multiple times by Muslim rulers. The most recent destruction was ordered by Mughal Emperor Aurangzeb in 1669 CE, who constructed a mosque (Gyanvapi Mosque) at the site.\n\nThe present Kashi Vishwanath temple was built by the Maratha queen Ahilyabai Holkar of Indore in 1780 CE on land adjacent to the Gyanvapi Mosque. Maharaja Ranjit Singh of Punjab donated 22 maunds (about 990 kg) of gold for the gilding of the temple spire in 1853 CE. Additional renovations were made by various rulers over the years. In 2021, the Kashi Vishwanath Corridor was inaugurated by Prime Minister Narendra Modi, creating a 5.4-lakh sq ft complex connecting the temple to the Ganga riverfront.\n\nThe new Kashi Vishwanath Dham corridor has transformed the pilgrimage experience. The project involved demolishing or relocating structures that blocked the path between the temple and the Ganga. The corridor now allows pilgrims to walk from the sacred Ganga ghats directly to the Vishwanath temple in a magnificent processional path, having taken a holy dip in the Ganga before darshan.',
