    story:
      'Kashi (Varanasi) is described in the Puranas as the eternal city that exists beyond the ordinary planes of existence. According to the Skanda Purana, Kashi is the cosmic center of the universe, situated on the trident of Lord Shiva. When the great deluge (pralaya) comes at the end of a cosmic cycle and all the worlds are destroyed, Kashi alone remains, held aloft on Shiva\'s trident, untouched by destruction. This is why Kashi is called the "City of Liberation" – those who die within its sacred boundaries are believed to receive the Taraka Mantra (liberation mantra) whispered in their ear by Shiva himself, thus attaining moksha regardless of their karma.\n\nThe name Vishwanath means "Lord of the Universe" – Vishwa (universe) + Nath (Lord). The Jyotirlinga here is believed to be the cosmic pillar of light that manifested to resolve the argument between Brahma and Vishnu about who was supreme. When both were unable to find the ends of this pillar of light, they understood it to be Shiva – the infinite, beginningless, and endless Absolute. This Jyotirlinga, therefore, represents the infinite nature of Shiva.\n\nThe sacred Ganga River flows northward at Varanasi (ordinarily rivers flow south), and this reversal is itself considered a divine phenomenon caused by the presence of Shiva in Kashi. The 84 ghats (stepped riverbanks) of Varanasi, with Dashashwamedh Ghat at the center, are the site of daily fire rituals (Ganga Aarti) that have been performed for centuries without interruption. Brahma is said to have performed the Dashashwamedha Yajna (the great horse sacrifice ritual) at this ghat, which is how it got its name.\n\nThe medieval history of Kashi Vishwanath is a story of repeated destruction and reconstruction that exemplifies the resilience of Hindu faith. The original temples at Kashi were destroyed by Qutb-ud-din Aibak in 1194 CE, then rebuilt by Iltutmish. The temple was then destroyed again by Aurangzeb in 1669 CE, who built the Gyanvapi Mosque at the site. The devout Maratha Queen Ahilyabai Holkar, heartbroken by the state of Lord Vishwanath\'s abode, built the present temple in 1780 CE adjacent to the mosque. Her faith and determination ensured that the Lord remained close to his original abode. Today, the grand Kashi Vishwanath Corridor connects this eternal temple to the sacred Ganga, restoring the pilgrimage experience to its ancient glory.',
    significance:
      'Kashi Vishwanath is the premier pilgrimage site for Shaivites. The city of Varanasi itself is considered Shiva\'s eternal abode. A pilgrimage to Kashi is believed to be the most direct path to liberation (moksha), as Shiva himself whispers the Taraka Mantra to those who die within the city\'s sacred boundaries. The 12 Jyotirlingas all lead to Kashi, but Kashi Vishwanath is the ultimate.',
