    story:
      'The story of Trimbakeshwar Jyotirlinga is rooted in the divine origin of the Godavari River. In ancient times, the great sage Gautama Rishi and his wife Ahalya lived on the Brahmagiri mountain near what is now Trimbak. Due to their severe penance and righteousness, Brahmagiri was transformed into a land of plenty even during a severe drought, attracting other sages to take shelter there.\n\nJealous of Gautama\'s fame and success, some envious sages devised a plot. They employed a divine cow (which turned out to be a manifestation of the goddess or a divine trick) to enter Gautama\'s crop fields. When Gautama tried to shoo the cow away using some kusha grass, the cow died – whether from a touch or from the force, accounts differ. The sages then accused Gautama of the heinous sin of go-hatya (killing of a cow). Though he maintained his innocence, Gautama accepted penance as prescribed and went on a pilgrimage to wash away this sin.\n\nOn the advice of the sages, Gautama prayed to Lord Shiva to bring the sacred Ganga to Brahmagiri mountain, so that he could take a holy bath and be purified. Shiva, pleased with Gautama\'s penance, asked the Ganga to flow to Brahmagiri. However, Ganga was reluctant to come to the earth again (having already done so when summoned by Bhagiratha for the Pandava ancestors). After much persuasion by Shiva and Gautama, Ganga agreed to manifest at Brahmagiri as the Godavari River, which is therefore also called Ganga and is considered equally sacred. The spot where the Godavari emerges from Brahmagiri mountain is considered the origin of the river.\n\nGrateful for Shiva\'s grace, Gautama and the assembled sages requested Lord Shiva to remain at Brahmagiri permanently. Shiva agreed and manifested as the Trimbakeshwar Jyotirlinga. The name Trimbakeshwar comes from Trimbaka, meaning "three-eyed" (tri = three, ambaka = eye) – an epithet of Shiva, the third eye being his eye of wisdom that destroys ignorance. The three-faced Shivalinga here represents the Trinity of Brahma, Vishnu, and Shiva unified in one divine form, symbolizing that Shiva as the Absolute encompasses all three aspects of the divine.',
