      'The Vaidyanath temple has been mentioned in ancient texts including the Ramayana and Mahabharata. Deoghar has been an important pilgrimage center for over 2,000 years. The temple complex here is unique – it consists of the main Vaidyanath temple (Jyotirlinga) surrounded by 21 subsidiary temples, creating one of the largest temple complexes in India.\n\nThe temple was patronized by various rulers over the centuries. The Gidhour kings and later the Gidhaur zamindars were traditional managers of the temple. During the medieval period, the temple was attacked and looted by Muslim rulers, but the Shivalinga survived. The Shravani Mela tradition is deeply ancient and is one of the most extraordinary acts of mass devotion in the world.\n\nThe Shravani Mela: During the month of Shravan (July–August), millions of devotees (called Kanwariyas or Bol-Bams) walk barefoot from Sultanganj in Bihar (about 105 km one way) carrying sacred Gangajal in clay pots on a bamboo pole (kanwar) on their shoulders. They offer this Gangajal at the Vaidyanath Shivalinga. The saffron-clad Kanwariyas create a river of faith on the road to Deoghar, an extraordinary spectacle of devotion.',
