    story:
      'The story of Ramanathaswamy Jyotirlinga is inseparably linked to the great epic Ramayana. After Lord Rama killed the demon king Ravana to rescue his wife Sita from Lanka, he returned to India accompanied by Sita, Lakshmana, and his army of Vanaras (monkeys). As Rama was about to return, the sages reminded him of an important matter: he had slain Ravana, who despite being a demon, was a Brahmin scholar and devotee of Shiva. The sin of Brahma-hatya (killing a Brahmin) is considered very grave in Hindu dharma, even when the Brahmin has committed grave sins. Rama needed to atone for this sin.\n\nThe sages advised Rama to worship Lord Shiva at Rameswaram to obtain absolution. Rama, accompanied by Sita and Lakshmana, came to Rameswaram (then an island at the southern tip of India). He decided to worship a Shivalinga for this purpose. He sent Hanuman (the devotee son of the wind god) to Kashi (Varanasi) to bring a sacred Shivalinga from there, as a Kashi Shivalinga would be especially powerful for atonement.\n\nHanuman went to Kashi but was delayed. The auspicious muhurta (moment) for the puja was approaching, and Hanuman had not returned. Sita therefore quickly made a Shivalinga from the sand of the beach (Valilingeswarar) and Rama worshipped it. When Hanuman returned with the Kashi Shivalinga, he was hurt that his Shivalinga was not used. Rama, to console Hanuman, placed the Kashi Shivalinga (now called Viswalingam or Hanuman Lingam) next to Sita\'s sand lingam and declared that both would be worshipped, but the Viswalingam would be worshipped first. Both Shivalingas are present in the temple today, and even today, worshippers offer puja to the Viswalingam first.\n\nRama also completed the 22 theerthams (sacred wells) at Rameswaram, each with a unique significance. When Rama prayed to the ocean, the ocean god appeared and suggested that Rama build the famous Ram Setu (Adam\'s Bridge) to reach Lanka. The ocean allowed the stones inscribed with Rama\'s name to float on the water, enabling the Vanara army to build a bridge to Lanka. Parts of this ancient bridge between India and Sri Lanka are still visible in satellite imagery. The entire island of Rameswaram is thus soaked in the divine presence of Rama, and the Shivalinga worshipped by Rama himself became the most powerful Jyotirlinga in the south.',
