    history:
      'The Grishneshwar temple was rebuilt by Queen Ahilyabai Holkar of Indore in the 18th century, the same queen who rebuilt the Kashi Vishwanath temple in Varanasi. The present temple dates to 1730 CE. The original temple was destroyed by Aurangzeb\'s forces in the 17th century.\n\nThe temple\'s proximity to the Ellora Caves (about 1.5 km away) makes it a unique combination of religious pilgrimage and historical/archaeological tourism. The Ellora Caves, built between the 6th and 11th centuries CE, contain remarkable rock-cut temples representing Buddhist, Hindu, and Jain traditions. The Kailash Temple (Cave 16) at Ellora, dedicated to Lord Shiva, is one of the greatest architectural achievements in human history and is a fitting companion site to the Grishneshwar Jyotirlinga nearby.\n\nThe temple has a rich tradition of Shaivite worship and has been an important pilgrimage center for the Maratha community. The region of Aurangabad (now Chhatrapati Sambhajinagar) in which it is located has been an important center of both Hindu and Islamic civilization, representing the complex cultural history of the Deccan region.',
