    story:
      'The story of Grishneshwar Jyotirlinga involves an extraordinary tale of devotion and divine justice from the Shiva Purana. In a village called Devagiri (which may be associated with Daulatabad near Ellora), there lived a devout Brahmin woman named Kusuma. She was the most ardent devotee of Lord Shiva, worshipping a Shivalinga made of sand every day by the pond. Through her sincere devotion, Shiva granted her a boon – every time she submerged the Shivalinga in the pond after worship, the lingam would re-emerge renewed, symbolizing Shiva\'s eternal presence.\n\nKusuma\'s devotion bore fruit: she was blessed with extraordinary good fortune. However, her co-wife (the Brahmin had two wives) Ghushma (also called Ghushna) was deeply jealous of Kusuma\'s devotion and the blessings she received. This jealousy festered into hatred. Ghushma devised a wicked plan – she killed Kusuma\'s son, dismembered the body, and secretly threw the pieces into the same pond where Kusuma performed her daily worship.\n\nWhen Kusuma discovered that her son was missing and later learned of the horrific act, she was devastated. However, instead of giving in to grief or anger, she continued her devotion to Lord Shiva with complete faith. The next day during her worship, when she submerged the Shivalinga in the pond as was her practice, her son emerged from the water alive and restored – Shiva had resurrected him. At that moment, Lord Shiva appeared before Kusuma in all his divine glory.\n\nLord Shiva, pleased with Kusuma\'s extraordinary equanimity and devotion even in the face of tragedy, offered to punish Ghushma for her terrible act. Kusuma, demonstrating the highest quality of spiritual forgiveness, requested Shiva to forgive Ghushma. Touched by this magnanimity, Shiva agreed to forgive Ghushma and established his permanent presence at that spot as Grishneshwar (also Ghushmeshwar or Ghrishneshwar). Some accounts say the name comes from Ghushma (the name of one of the women in the story, symbolizing that even sinners can be redeemed through proximity to divine grace), while others say it comes from the Sanskrit root "Ghrishn" meaning "one who shines brilliantly" – referring to the blazing Jyotirlinga.',
